[{:seon.task/name "config-is-schema-defaults-and-datoms"
  :seon.task/plan "PLAN, approved by root 2026-11-16 (the owner: \"FIX IT NOW\"; one shape). NEXT: build step (a), the datom step, and READY it; the role seam waits for flow's answer to runtime's question (what carries a process's role and facts; does its rebuild already derive config). TARGET: each setting is a datom on the branch; a process's config is derived from those datoms and moves with each commit that touches one; a read picks by the process's role, read through one function whose source is flow's start args (decisions 4ba04e9b40; never seon.agent/*start*, G2's var flow corrected) (no atom, no var re-rooting); a new key is a schema datom, so it needs no rebuild or restart. PRIOR WORK [read]: seon.config (built-text, layers-over, read-layer over aero, layered, of-entries, entries, hold!, hold-args!, test-system-config, the held atom, the config/sources vars, may-be-absent gone, as-read); entries as text, one :config/key :config/form per key, written by seon.source/config-tx (the import of default.edn) and read by seon.program/hold-config!, config-refused and load-diff!, seon.merge's :config kind, seon.proofs, seon.agent/transact!; the schema registry from the branch (seon.program/schema-registry, schema-changes); the reactive core (seon.ratom: track, a reaction re-run when a datom it read moves); a process's role and facts arrive through its procs' start args (flow's rebuild; decisions 4ba04e9b40), read here through one function, the seam. MEASURED [M]: 21 direct readers of the config/sources vars in 10 files; 288 calls of value/resolve/in-force; 24 place keys, 33 at-load keys, 1 build key; 39 tagged keys in default.edn; a hold on a branch move (of-entries: aero read of the entries' text, validation, schema-of compile) ~6.7M ticks; a test child's hold 8.1M ticks / 23 ms (result/d10c5747); a layered read 0.66M. THE SHAPE: (1) VALUE DATOMS. A setting is {:config/key k :config/value v}, v the EDN value (sedn-decoded once at its write, never aero), on the branch. A key no datom states takes its schema entry's :default; its schema entry is a branch datom too (:schema/key, already). The import of default.edn and :config/form go. (2) PROCESS FACTS ARE START VALUES. What #env, #seon/checkout and #seon/cores give today (ports, store path, runs, main, source root, checkout paths, cores, the profile) are facts of a process, read from its argv and environment by one function (process-facts), no stored state; the role (agent, test child) arrives through flow's start args, read through one function (the seam, plugged in when flow answers). The 24 place keys become reads of process-facts. default.edn, aero for config, --config and --set go; config/local.edn's ports and source root become the standing system's start args. (3) PROFILES ARE DATOMS. A profile (the test profile: provider :scripted, its script, sole-writer? false, private-checked? true) is a set of {:config/profile p :config/key k :config/value v} datoms; a process's role (the seam) picks its profile. test-system-config goes: a process test reads the test profile's datoms like any read. (4) ONE DERIVED VALUE, A REACTION. A process's config = schema defaults, then the branch's value datoms, then its role's profile datoms: one seon.ratom track over the config datoms of its db and its role, re-run only when a commit touches a :config/* or a config :schema/* datom (O(d)). (cfg/value k) derefs it: a map get. The held atom, hold!, hold-args!, the alter-var-roots, the config and sources vars and their 21 readers (onto cfg/value) go. A test child's role is test (the seam); it parses nothing, installs nothing. (5) THE SCHEMA FROM THE BRANCH. The config map and its defaults are read from the branch's schema datoms (seon.program/schema-registry), never the baked seon.config*.edn: a new key is a schema datom on the branch, live at its commit; no rebuild, no restart. The 33 at-load keys are read where they are used (a Flow buffer size at the proc's start reads the config at that start); the at-load and waiting arms go. The 1 build key (jolt compiler options) stays a build input, read by the build. DELETES: aero's config reading (built-text, layers-over, read-layer, layered's layers, overrides, profile, argv/parse-set), default.edn (its 3 keys bin/env and bin/tree sed stay in a build-input file until those scripts go), :config/form and seon.source/config-tx's text import, of-entries, entries-text, text-refusal, hold!/hold-args!, the held atom, the config and sources vars, test-system-config, the place, at-load and waiting arms, the gate-checkout derivation (flow's landing), seon.agent/hold-env!'s config part. COST BOUND (arithmetic): a read: one deref and one map get, ~200 ticks; a commit that touches config: the reaction re-run over the ~220 keys' datoms (an AEVT read of :config/value ~0.2M, the schema defaults from the registry it already holds) ~0.3M, against ~6.7M a hold today; a commit that touches none: 0; a test child: 0 (its role picks its profile); a new key: one schema datom and one value datom, no build. ORDER, each landing with its first caller: (a) value datoms and the reaction behind cfg/value, the 21 var readers moved onto it, the held atom and holds deleted; (b) process facts into the start value, default.edn's tags and aero deleted; (c) profiles as datoms, test-system-config and hold-env!'s config deleted; (d) the schema from the branch, the at-load and waiting arms deleted. Paths each runs on: the standing system's boot and update, agents, test children, the candidate binary's boot, the merge (:config kind becomes plain datoms).  STATUS (runtime, 2026-11-16): on main: default.edn's plain values are their schema entries' :default, applied by malli's default-value-transformer in seon.config/resolved; the config map is open; a key is required unless marked {:optional true}, a default counting toward it; 39 keys stay in the file (tagged values, the #ref targets, three keys bin/env and bin/tree sed). The interim test-child hand-over (27a9c81574, test-held! in the held atom) was reverted unmerged (1f4d9e88d8): it built on the held atom, aero's layers and the checkout arm this task deletes. NEXT: step (a). The role seam waits on flow's start map (no :role on main or jolt/flow yet); (a) does not need it."--profile" "scripted"] is 8.1M ticks / 10.3 MB / 23 ms in every test child (was ~175 ms before step 1b shrank default.edn from 221 keys to 39); its parts: the entries read 86k, the schema changes 0.76M, a layered read 1.4M, and of-entries' second aero read, layered and schema-of compile the rest. Every child computes the same value: its agent's config under the test profile. PLAN: (test-config es changes), the pure core of hold-args! (no atom, no alter-var-root), answers the test profile's config as a value; the agent keeps it derived from what it is derived from (its entries and schema changes), computed once a config move; the child installs it at its start (one reset), parsing and validating nothing. BOUND: a child ~10k ticks and 0 bytes; the agent ~8.1M once a config move, not once a child. PRIOR WORK: seon.config/hold-args!, of-entries, layered, hold!; seon.agent/hold-env!. It is step 4's first half (a test child takes its parent's config); step 2 (config-of from datoms, no held atom) follows. NEXT: steps 2-4. Step 1a done on jolt/runtime 3a2b4d63f0, not on main: a schema's defaults reach every config through layered (since moved onto malli's default-value-transformer), proof seon.config-test/a-key-no-layer-states-resolves-to-its-schemas-default red on main's layered (2 of 6) and green (6), 16.9k ticks over 221 entries; it is READY only together with step 1b, its first use. NEXT: step 1b, then 2-4. What the survey found [M, main 3a7c573541]: default.edn holds 221 keys; 186 are plain values; ~25 are process facts (#env, #seon/checkout, and #ref/#join paths built from them: ports, store, runs, source root, checkout, results, program store); 6 are #profile values (sole-writer?, prove-main-on-commit, ai/provider, ai/script, private-checked?, ...) that a test child holds as the test profile's datoms. ORDER, each landing with its first caller (a capability never lands without one): (1) the 186 plain values move from default.edn into their schema entries as :default, each with its provenance comment carried into the entry's description, and the built config reads its defaults from the schemas (malli's default-value-transformer over the registry's schema) in place of those lines: default.edn shrinks to the tagged keys; proof red first: every key the schemas default resolves to the value the file gave. (2) config-of: schema defaults, then the branch's config datoms, then the task's :seon.task/config datoms, as hold-args!'s resolution, with its proof of ~1 ms. (3) the process facts as one function of the process's start values (no aero #env). (4) the test profile as datoms, and a test child takes its parent's config: after flow's G2 (the start map's :cfg), hold-env! and aero go. Coordinate: data reads config through hold-main-schema! (its hold-config!); flow's G2 owns the start map. TOUCHPOINTS of step 1 [read, main 3a7c573541]: default.edn is read at start (seon.config/built-text, layers-over, read-layer under a profile), imported into one :config/key :config/form entry per key (the branch's config entries: seon.config/entries, of-entries), carried by the merge (seon.merge's :config kind), re-held when an entry moves (seon.program/load-diff! and hold-config!, seon.agent/transact!), and read from a git tree for build-input marks (seon.fx/config-input, its :seon.config/build, :at-load and :place marks, fx.clj:418). So a schema's :default must be the BOTTOM layer every reader resolves over (malli's default-value-transformer, applied in seon.config/resolved), and a key with no entry resolves to it. Owner, 2026-11-16: \"How is it possible reading a config can take so long? Move all config defaults into schemas and make adding a set of custom datoms for an agent launching, for it to have a custom config, a simple update.\" CAUSE [M]: a test child's first act, seon.agent/hold-env! -> seon.config/hold-args!, takes ~175 ms of its ~190 ms start (result/dcc6e70b, result/b7042bb7): it parses the binary's whole default.edn text (1,307 lines, 221 keys) with aero under a profile, resolving 21 #env and 6 #profile tags against the process environment (153 ms, 23M ticks warm, result/d2ee98d9), layers it, reads the branch's 218 config entries cold through the child's new store connection (337 ms wall at an agent, 6M ticks), and checks each against the config schema (63 ms). Every process repeats it for a map that is nearly always the same. PRIOR WORK: seon.config (586 lines: built-text, layers-over, read-layer over aero, layered, load-layers, of-entries, entries, hold!, hold-args!, profile, overrides, argv/parse-set), resources/seon/config/default.edn, the :seon.config.* schema entries (resources/seon/schemas/seon.config*.edn, each with description and unit, none with a default), a task's :seon.task/config (a task's own settings ride its task: docs/config.md#the-resolver), seon.agent/hold-env!, seon.fx/root-variables, this file's a-keys-levels-are-its-schemas. BOUND (arithmetic): a process's config = the program's schema defaults (one map of ~220 keys, derived once from the schema entries and kept with the compiled program, inherited by every fork: 0 a process) merged with the branch's config datoms (its :config entries, an AEVT read of ~220 small datoms, ~0.2M ticks warm) merged with the agent's task's datoms (:seon.task/config, a pull): ~0.3M ticks, under 1 ms, against ~175 ms. DESIGN, each deleting what it replaces: (1) each :seon.config.* schema entry states its default (:default), so the defaults are data in the schema; default.edn's values move there and the file goes, and with it aero, built-text, layers, read-layer, layered, load-layers and profile (a #profile value is a datom a profile's task or branch states). (2) the config in force is one function, (config-of db task): defaults-of-schema, then the branch's config datoms, then the task's :seon.task/config datoms; a custom config for an agent at launch is a few datoms on its task, a plain transact. (3) the 21 #env values are facts of a process, not config (its ports, store, runs, source root, checkout): root decides them at start and hands each process its own as values (its spawn's args), never read back through the environment; fx/root-variables and the env scrubbing in hold-env! go. (4) a test child holds its parent's config as the fork gives it, plus the test profile's datoms; hold-env! goes. ORDER: (1) and (2) first with their proof, then (3) (touches start and spawn: runtime with data), then (4). PROOFS, red first: a process's config is resolved within ~1 ms from the schema defaults and its branch's and task's datoms (red today: ~175 ms); an agent launched with a task carrying a config datom runs with that value; no file is read for config after boot."
